@startuml UiOutputVocabulary
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' purpose: the fixed status vocabulary proveo prints — six roles, three severities, one measured writer
' covers: internal/ui/**
' covers: e2e/stream_palette_test.go (whiteRun, proveoLines, TestWhiteRunSkipsSbxsOwnLines)
' covers: internal/sbx/host_ollama.go (pullOllama: the first user of the live line)
' covers: internal/sbx/host_ollama_pull_test.go
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/report.go (Item.Log, PrintBlock, Block)
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/log_moves_test.go
'   the language this obeys ....... _spec/_conventions/tui-design-language.puml
'   the other speaker ............. _spec/internal/choiceui/choice-prompt-render.puml
'   where the plain copy lands ..... _spec/internal/runlog/run-transcript.puml
'   the glyph tier's other consumer  _spec/internal/posture/one-value-two-renderings.puml
'   the strip grammar .............. _spec/internal/agentio/agent-terminal-io.puml (termtext)
'
' N5 (DONE (wt) 2026-10-17): the tee's stripper is termtext.StripANSI
' (internal/ui/ui.go:262), shared with agentio's tail and the e2e readers;
' ui's own stripANSI is deleted. It skipped from ESC to the next 'm', so a
' non-SGR sequence cost the log the text up to the next 'm' —
' "\x1b[2Khello mom" logged as "om" (measured by the coordinator before the
' fix). TestTheRunLogKeepsTextAfterANonSGRSequence pins the repair.
'
' ── WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY THE OLD NOTES HERE WERE WRONG ────────────────────
' This file used to describe five verbs and defend the emoji as deliberate:
' "the icon is replaced by a word, never dropped", with Iconf as the one
' exception whose decoration plain mode could discard. That was an accurate
' description of a design that did not match the rest of proveo.
'
' Two claims in particular did not survive contact with a screenshot of a real
' run. First, "Plain is a degradation, never a second design" was true of the
' three severity verbs and false of Iconf, which was the majority: 57 of the
' call sites, carrying 23 different emoji, and plain mode DROPPED that
' decoration rather than degrading it — so a log lost the only thing
' distinguishing an image line from a credential line. Second, the fancy
' rendering was never a rendering: line() emitted no ANSI. The palette
' had been sitting in this package since the banner needed it, consumed only by
' choiceui and WriteBrandBanner, while the stream that carries most of what an
' operator reads printed monochrome.
'
' ── EVERY OPERATOR-FACING LINE GOES THROUGH THE PRINTER (2026-09-27) ──────
' Swept cmd/proveo and internal for raw fmt.Print*: `ls`, `projects`,
' `setup --print`, the numbered target/scope pickers, the y/N and field prompts
' (init wizard, uninstall) and the Ollama pull now speak the Printer's verbs
' under a Section (R4 took the Section off `ls`, `projects`, `setup --print`
' and uninstall's question: WHAT THE LISTINGS PRINT below). Askf is the one new verb: a question that waits for input
' on the SAME line — "› " brand + bold text (ascii "> "), plain the bare text.
' A pull's live progress line only redraws on a TTY; plain gets start + end.
' DELIBERATELY PLAIN, because something copies or parses them: `version`
' (compared against HEAD), `run --print` and `build --print` plans
' (copy-pasteable commands), the schedule tick's launchd log, transcripts/traces,
' and in-container markers (PROVEO_SMOKE_READY). The hidden `targets` TSV was on
' this list until N9 deleted the command (2026-10-18).
'
' ── THE VERB SET IS NOW TWO AXES, NOT ONE LIST ─────────────────────────────
' Severity — is this fine, worth attention, or broken — and role — which layer
' is speaking. They are independent, so they are not one enumeration: Okf and
' Failf answer the first and say nothing about the second; Hostf and Appf answer
' the second and assert nothing about the first. Iconf tried to be the second
' axis by handing the call site a picture, which is why there were 23 of them.
'
' Notef is unchanged in meaning and changed in rendering: it is the continuation
' line under a preceding verb, and it now indents to that verb's TEXT column
' instead of to zero, so a warning's detail reads as part of the warning.

' ── WRAPPED LINES KEEP THEIR COLOUR (2026-10-26) ─────────────────────────
' wrapOne broke a grey line with "\n"+pad and no SGR, so each continuation
' showed default colour in logs and pagers (the Toolbox jetbrains:// link,
' long paths). It now re-emits activeSGR — every SGR since the last reset —
' after the pad. tmux capture-pane -e still omits an SGR that repeats the
' previous line's state, so the e2e whiteRun carries the open colour across
' lines, and it ends proveo's stretch at sbx 0.49's "  KITS"/"  SANDBOX"
' blocks. Tests: TestWrapCarriesTheColourOntoEachContinuation,
' TestWhiteRunSkipsSbxsOwnLines, TestStreamPaletteE2E.
'
' ── GREY DETAIL (2026-10-05) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
' Colouring only the mark left every line's TEXT in the terminal default, so a
' prelude read as a wall of white. Signal-only rendering: role lines (● host,
' app, store …) and Notef print fully ColorMuted #8A8A8A; a ✓ ⚠ × line keeps
' its HEAD in the terminal default — the text before its first short
' "label: " (≤ 40 cols) or " — " — and greys the rest. A × head is bold.
' Default-colour text therefore means "a severity spoke"; a clean run is grey.
' Rules drop to ColorRule #585858 with a bold ColorSecondary label.
' ColorSecondary keeps its value: choiceui, help and schedule share it.
' Chosen over "muted" (every head default) and "ledger" (grey key column):
' see the comparison artifact https://claude.ai/artifact/7HGBovkYAAkChBt6P9V7cz
' Words wider than the line hard-break at the text column instead of letting
' the terminal wrap them at column zero.
' Verified by e2e/stream_palette_test.go: one real run in tmux asserting the
' grey, rule and bold-failure SGRs and ≤ 2 consecutive default-colour lines.
'
' ── FACTS AND DETAIL (2026-11-11, R0 of _spec/_plans/output-readability.puml)
' The operator compared sbx's create block with proveo's stream and chose to
' adopt its principles, keeping proveo's ● dots ("Keep ● dots, aligned").
' ui.Facts prints facts about one subject as ONE block:
'   ● checkout: ~/w/hotfix (linked worktree)
'               cloned from ~/w/main                  ← Detail, dim
'   ● .env:     ~/w/main/.env (copy, rw)
'               ~/w/main/.env.local (copy, rw)        ← a row with no Key
'   ● links:    _spec → ~/Syncd/_spec
' Each keyed row keeps its role's dot colour; key and value print grey as a
' role line always has, so GREY DETAIL holds: no default-colour text, and a
' clean run stays grey. Keys are lowercase and end in ":". Values start in
' one column per block: the widest key, its colon, one space. A row with no
' Key stacks its Value under the row above, with no dot. Detail lines sit at
' the value column in ColorRule #585858; under a value in a stack they sit
' two columns further, so plain output (no shade) cannot read a detail as one
' more value of the list (R3: "later:" stacked "prompt", "credentials" and
' credentials' how-to in one column). A row with no Value and no Detail
' prints nothing, so a caller passes every fact and lets the empty ones drop;
' such a row widens no key column either (R1: the run block's empty "scope"
' padded "log:" to three spaces).
' PLAIN MODE drops the mark column and keeps the alignment: "log:   /x".
' A VALUE WRAPS AT ITS OWN COLUMN on a terminal. wrapWriter.writeHang takes
' the hanging column; multi forwards it to the terminal half alone, so the
' log copy keeps one line per row (TestTeeDoesNotWrapTheLog's rule).
' ui.Detailf writes a dim line two columns past the text column, under a
' ✓ ⚠ × item. ui.Tilde writes a host path under $HOME as "~/…"; it replaced
' schedule's private tildeHome.
' Pinned by internal/ui/facts_test.go:
'   TestFactsAlignValuesInOneColumnAndKeepTheRoleDot
'   TestPlainFactsAlignWithoutMarks
'   TestAFactValueWrapsAtItsOwnColumnAndTheLogStaysOneLine
'   TestDetailfIndentsTwoPastTheTextColumn
'   TestTildeAbbreviatesOnlyUnderHome
'   TestARowThatPrintsNothingWidensNoKeyColumn
'   TestFactsWithNothingToPrintDrawNoHeading: a block whose rows are all empty
'     draws no pending heading either
'   TestTildeInAbbreviatesEachHomePathInsideText: ui.TildeIn rewrites each home
'     path that starts inside a line (an error's text, "link → target"); a
'     path that only contains the home string mid-word stays as it was
' Pinned without sbx by e2e/stream_palette_test.go TestWhiteRunSkipsSbxsOwnLines:
' a fact block, a ✓ outcome with dim detail, and the interface block after
' sbx's create block count 0 white lines.
' THE TEE KEEPS THE SCREEN'S "~/". A Facts row reaches the run log as the
' screen drew it, so a teed row carries "~/"; rows written with ui.Logf (the
' create block's facts, the IDE block, the posture) stay absolute. The log's
' header records "home: <absolute $HOME>" once, so each "~/" resolves (the
' operator, 2026-11-11, decision C; _spec/internal/runlog/run-transcript.puml).
' OUTCOME LINES (the operator, 2026-11-11, decision A; amends P7 of
' _spec/_plans/output-readability.puml). A block ends on a ✓/⚠/× line only
' when it reaches a verdict its facts do not already state:
'   workspace    ends on ✓ "private clone — …" or ✓ "mounted checkout — …"
'   credentials  ends on its ⚠/× items when one exists, else on its facts
'   run, egress  facts only; no outcome line
'   results      ends on the sandbox's fate (R2): ✓ <name> — removed, or the
'                ⚠ kept line, which the "ide" block then follows
' A fact block never repeats itself as a ✓ line. NOT CHOSEN: an outcome line
' on every block; a credentials ✓ line in place of the "login" fact.
' sandbox.PrintBlock prints facts, then items, so an item is the last line.
' ITEM LOG LINES. sandbox.Item.Log holds the why and the how-to-change of a
' ⚠ or ● item; Item.print writes them with ui.Logf, so they reach the run log
' and never the screen (P6): the re-attach's "workspace block is this run's
' plan", the buildx builder knob, the sbx secret store's how-to.
'
' ── THE END OF A RUN (2026-11-11, R2 of _spec/_plans/output-readability.puml)
' sandbox.Block gathers one subject's facts and items while its steps run and
' prints them once (PrintBlock); a row added after Print prints at once. The
' results block of _spec/internal/backend/teardown.puml is its first user.
' Block.Take hands what Print would print to a caller that prints it its own
' way, and counts as Print: init's revisit drops what it printed before
' (_spec/cmd/proveo/init-sbx-bootstrap.puml). ui.WithdrawSection withdraws
' a heading declared and not drawn, so a subject a revisit left unchanged
' does not lend its heading to the next live line, which Printer.Live draws
' (TestAWithdrawnSectionDrawsNothing).
' SectionIDE ("ide") heads a kept sandbox's attach facts; it prints only when
' the sandbox stays, after results, so nothing redraws "results" after it.
' Item gained Ok (✓) and Fail (×): a closing "✓ <name> — removed" is a
' verdict no fact states (decision A), and "× sbx run — exited with code N"
' prints only when sbx said nothing itself (P9).
' ui.LogWriter is a writer whose every line reaches the run log alone: the
' Hermes Desktop install.sh output goes there.
' A WRAPPED FACT KEEPS ITS COLUMN. wrapOne used to rejoin words with one
' space, so a wrapped row lost the gap that aligns its value ("vs code:
' code …"), and a word wider than the line moved to its own row first,
' leaving "toolbox:" bare. It now keeps each whitespace run between words
' it does not break at, and a word no fresh line can hold breaks where it
' starts (MEASURED on the kept ide block, TestStreamPaletteE2E's capture).
' Pinned by TestAWrappedFactKeepsItsAlignedGapAndBreaksALongWordAtItsOwnColumn
' and TestLogWriterSendsEachLineToTheRunLogAlone.
'
' ── WHAT INIT PRINTS (2026-11-11, R3 of _spec/_plans/output-readability.puml)
' `proveo init` names its subjects: sbx · prompt · git · credentials · operator
' · results, each once, each after the step that makes it true. SectionPrompt
' ("prompt") and SectionGit ("git") are new; SectionExecution, SectionStarting
' and SectionSecrets lost their last callers and left: eighteen sections.
' OUTCOME LINES, init's (decision A applied): sbx, prompt, git, credentials and
' operator are facts with their ⚠/× items; results ends on ONE closing line
' for a verdict no fact states: "✓ host — ready to run", "× host — not ready
' to run", "⚠ init — quit at page n/N", "⚠ init — cancelled at the checks
' page", "⚠ init — stopped at <row>" (a program init handed the terminal to
' failed and printed its own error, P9 and R2's F3), "× <row> — failed" (a
' failure only proveo saw), or "× init — stopped" (an error no step names:
' the setup page's own form error, no plan for a prefix it chose), the error
' dim. After a verify that found the host ready, each ending but ✓ keeps "host
' ready to run" as its last dim line. Every one but ✓ returns
' backend.Reported, or a Stopped proveo re-raises after a Ctrl+C in a
' hand-off, so main adds no second ×. A fact never
' repeats as a ✓: the operator's name, the git identity and a stored key are
' facts; "done:" left (it was false three ways).
' Steps.Quiet and Steps.Silent: _spec/internal/ui/step-lines.puml.
' init writes no run log, so ui.Logf is a no-op there; nothing init used to
' print moved to Logf. Its how-tos went to `proveo init --help` (F2).
'
' ── WHAT THE LISTINGS PRINT (2026-11-11, R4 of _spec/_plans/output-readability.puml)
' A one-subject command draws NO heading (the operator: rm, adopt, attach,
' clean, ls, projects, the schedule listing, setup, update, uninstall). `proveo
' run` with no target keeps "running" and "targets"; run and init keep theirs.
' SectionProjects, SectionSchedule, SectionSetup and SectionUninstall lost
' their last callers and left: fourteen sections. SectionTargets and
' SectionRunning now head only `proveo run`'s two-block listing.
'   ls        ONE Facts block, app role: "<target>: <image>"
'   projects  one store-role row per member path; no tool column (P6, P9)
'   schedule  ONE Facts block keyed by job, scheduler role; × items after it;
'             ONE closing tick line ("✓ tick — installed", the manager dim)
'   attach    (no terminal) ONE Facts block keyed by task, the same running
'             value as the schedule listing, no literal "●" in the value;
'             one dim how-to on stderr; a refusal, "⚠ attach — stopped at
'             tmux" or "● tmux — nested read-only client" otherwise
'   rm        a kept sandbox is a P2 item "<name> — <why>", its age dim
'             ("19h 7m ago", ui.Span: the schedule's wait form, pinned by
'             TestSpanWritesAWaitOrAnAgeToTheMinute); one
'             closing line: "✓ <name> — removed | already gone", "× <name>
'             — not removed" with its why and ONE fix dim, "⚠ <name> — no
'             such sandbox" (exit 0, HEAD's status)
'   durations a wait or an age reads ui.Span ("in 1d 23h", "19h 7m ago"); a
'             budget and the clock against it read as schedule.yml writes a
'             budget ("1h7m of 1h30m" beside "1h30m budget"). NOT CHOSEN:
'             ui.Span for both ("of 1h 0m" beside "budget: 1h")
'   adopt     ONE "to adopt" fact, stacked; "✓ branch <b> — created at <ref>"
'   clean     ONE Facts block by kind, error role (red without a failure),
'             printed after the removals; ⚠ items; one closing
'   setup     ONE item, init's PATH item word for word: "✓ ~/.proveo/bin —
'             added to PATH in ~/.zshrc", "⚠ … — not on this shell's PATH",
'             "× ~/.zshrc — not written"; the line to add dim
'   update    a live line per wait (latest.json, the download, uninstall.sh);
'             "✓ proveo 0.3.1 → 0.4.0 — updated", "● … — available" for
'             --check, "× latest.json — not read | not valid", the cause dim
'   uninstall the question names each part found; ONE Facts block of what
'             left, error role (root · path · prompt · font); ⚠ for each
'             removal that failed and each thing that stays; "✓ proveo —
'             uninstalled" or "⚠ proveo — uninstalled; N left"
' (_spec/internal/results/result-store.puml, _spec/internal/clean/clean-lifecycle.puml,
' _spec/internal/shell/setup-path.puml, _spec/internal/cdn/distribution-update.puml.)
' Dangerf lost its last caller (uninstall's red line, P9) and left ui.go: the
' error role prints as a Fact's Role or through Rolef, so five roles carry a
' verb and RoleError carries none (internal/contract TestEveryRoleVerbIsWired).
' sandbox.Item splits a detail at each newline, so an error git or sbx wrote
' over several lines keeps the detail column (FOUND 9 of the R4 kept design,
' read from code: Detailf wrote the later lines at column 0); blank rows drop.
' An absence is one role line on stderr ("● nothing running") and stdout
' stays empty. A command's refusal is ONE × item with ONE dim line, printed
' by the command through sandbox.PrintBlock under no heading (cmd/proveo
' reported), returning backend.Reported: main adds no second × (R3's
' mechanism, settled). `proveo version`, the tick's launchd log and
' `schedule run --print` stay DELIBERATELY PLAIN; the tick log names a job
' still running once.
'
' ── WHICH SCREEN LINES ARE PROVEO'S (2026-10-20) ───────────────────────────
' MEASURED: the newer sbx prints its own uncoloured lines into the same pane —
' a `── RESOLVE SETUP` block, `⚠ WARNING: template … built for the "shell"
' agent`, `warning: passing a sandbox kit through --kit is deprecated` — and
' the entrypoint banner follows. whiteRun skipped only `agent:` lines, so it
' counted all of those as proveo's and the ≤ 2 guard failed on sbx's text.
' proveoLines now keeps only stretches the Printer wrote. A stretch OPENS at
' the top of the screen and at every section heading
' (`^\s*[─-]{6} <lowercase label> [─-]+$`; sbx's `── RESOLVE SETUP` has two
' rules and an upper-case label). It CLOSES at the first non-blank line that
' does not open with a Printer mark or the text-column indent
' ("  ", "● ", "✓ ", "⚠ ", "× ", "› " on the nerd tier the test runs at).
' So the prelude counts up to sbx's first line, the results heading reopens
' the stream after the agent, and PROVEO_EXIT closes it. Raw sbx output
' inside the prelude (`sbx template load`, written straight to stderr) also
' closes it until proveo's next heading.
' Pinned without sbx by TestWhiteRunSkipsSbxsOwnLines (same file): sbx lines
' count 0, three default-colour proveo lines still count 3 in the prelude, in
' results, and after a heading that follows template-load output.

' ── ONE LIVE LINE, ON THE TERMINAL ALONE (2026-11-10) ─────────────────────
' The Printer had no way to draw something that changes in place. The one
' caller that needed it, the Ollama pull, wrote its frames straight to
' Printer.W. After TeeTo that writer is the terminal AND the run log, so each
' frame reached the log with its carriage return (probe: 3 frames, 4 CRs). W
' also wraps to the width, so a wide frame was split into two rows and could
' no longer be erased (_spec/_experiments/terminal-handoff-probes.puml, C2).
' Live(role, format, …) draws one line that the next frame replaces:
'   it writes to the terminal alone (Printer.term), never to a tee
'   the frame is cut to the terminal's width, less one column, so it stays
'     on one row and `CR, erase to end of line` removes it
'   an ordinary line erases the frame, prints, and redraws the frame under
'     it, all under the Printer's own lock
'   a question (Askf) ends it; EndLive erases it
'   a plain printer (a pipe, a file, NO_COLOR, TERM=dumb) draws nothing
' The log therefore holds the lines and never a frame. pullOllama is the
' first caller: its `!p.Plain` test and its three raw writes are gone.
' THIS IS ONE LINE, NOT A REGION. Teardown's running step is its second
' caller (_spec/internal/backend/teardown.puml, T6): one frame per step,
' redrawn on a one-second clock. init's apply and a run's start followed,
' through ui.Steps. SuspendLive takes the line off while a prompt has the
' terminal: _spec/internal/ui/step-lines.puml.
' ui.Fancy() tells a caller whether Default can redraw, so it can print a
' plain start line where it cannot. ui.NewTerminal(w, cols) is a Printer that
' treats w as a terminal of that width; tests in other packages read frames
' off it.
' Pinned by internal/ui/live_test.go:
'   TestALiveFrameReachesTheTerminalAndNeverTheLog
'   TestAnOrdinaryLineErasesTheLiveFrameAndRedrawsItBelow
'   TestALiveFrameIsCutToTheTerminalsWidth
'   TestAPlainPrinterDrawsNoLiveFrame
'   TestAQuestionEndsTheLiveLine
' and by internal/sbx/host_ollama_pull_test.go:
'   TestAPullWritesNoProgressFrameIntoAPlainStream
'   TestAFailedPullReturnsItsErrorAndLeavesNoFrame
'   TestAPullsStartAndEndReachTheLogAndNotTheScreen (R2: the pull is host
'     preparation; its start, forecast and "pulled" line go to the run log,
'     a plain printer keeps one start line, and egress's "local model" fact
'     gains a dim "pulled this run · 4.0 GiB")
'
title Output vocabulary — six roles, three severities, fourteen sections, one measured writer

component "Printer{W, Plain, Tier}" as P <<app>>
component "Hostf · Appf · Asyncf\nCloudf · Storef · Fact.Role\n— role: which layer speaks" as ROLES <<app>>
component "Okf · Warnf · Failf\n— severity: fine · attention · broken" as SEV <<app>>
component "Notef\ncontinuation, indented\nto the text column" as NOTE <<app>>
component "Askf\nquestion, same line,\nawaits input" as ASK <<app>>
component "Section(ui.Section*)\nfourteen closed names\nheading drawn LAZILY" as SEC <<app>>
component "line(role, sev, format)\nthe ONE writer" as LINE <<app>>
component "wrapTo(width)\nhanging indent at the\ntext column" as WRAP <<app>>
component "isFancy(w)\nTTY · NO_COLOR · TERM=dumb" as FANCY <<host>>
component "termWidth(w)\nioctl, else 100" as WIDTH <<host>>
component "GlyphTier\nPROVEO_GLYPHS\nNerd · Safe · ASCII · Off" as TIER <<host>>
component "termtext.StripANSI" as STRIP <<app>>
component "Default → os.Stderr" as DEF <<app>>
component "TeeTo(w)\nrun log" as TEE <<app>>

database "terminal\n● role colour · ✓ ⚠ ×\nwrapped to width" as TERM <<db>>
database "log file · pipe · CI\nok: · warn: · error:\nno colour, no glyph" as LOG <<db>>

ROLES ARROW_MAIN LINE
SEV   ARROW_MAIN LINE
NOTE  ARROW_MAIN LINE
SEC   ARROW_OPTIONAL LINE : pending until a line arrives
LINE ARROW_MAIN FANCY : which rendering
LINE ARROW_MAIN TIER : which rune
LINE ARROW_MAIN WRAP
WRAP ARROW_MAIN WIDTH
FANCY ARROW_MAIN TERM : fancy
FANCY ARROW_OPTIONAL LOG : plain
DEF ARROW_MAIN LINE
TEE ARROW_MAIN STRIP
STRIP ARROW_MAIN LOG

note right of ROLES
  **A role is named; a glyph never is.** Six roles, the
  identity's own — so a component drawn ""<<host>>"" in
  this tree prints host-cyan in a terminal. The mark is
  the role's dot, ""●"", in every case: what varies is
  the colour, because a second geometric shape per role
  would be a code the reader has to learn.

  A ""RoleError"" fact: the error role WITHOUT a
  failure — what ""proveo clean"" or ""proveo
  uninstall"" removed. Red for destructive,
  ""●"" for nothing went wrong.
end note

note bottom of SEV
  **Three severities: one-column glyph, theme colour.**
  ""✓"" · ""⚠"" · ""×"" on the nerd tier, ""+"" · ""!"" · ""x"" on
  ASCII, ""ok: "" · ""warn: "" · ""error: "" in plain — painted
  <color:#009532>#009532</color> · <color:#818D00>#CBDB2A</color> · <color:#CB2000>#CB2000</color>, the identity's own
  success green, attention lime and error red.

  The green arrived with this pass. This package had
  mirrored the six ROLE colours and no others, so the ok
  mark was teal for want of a green, and a codebase with
  no green in reach reaches for U+2705 instead — already
  coloured, already the wrong width. The colour is the
  fix; the glyph never needed to carry it.

  The U+FE0F that used to ride the warn mark is gone:
  go-runewidth measures the variation selector as one
  column and terminals draw the pair as two, which is
  the whole reason this stream could not wrap itself.
end note

note right of SEC
  **A divider names a subject, in one lowercase word.**
  A run reads ""run"", ""credentials"", ""egress"",
  ""workspace"", ""sbx"", ""interface"", ""results"",
  and ""ide"" when the sandbox stays, each after the
  step that makes its lines true, and each once in
  a run (teardown's checks print in ""results"").

  Drawn by the first line that FOLLOWS it, never by the
  call that declares it — a prelude is almost all
  conditional lines, and a stage with nothing to say
  must leave no heading behind. Re-declaring the current
  heading continues its block. A live step draws a
  pending heading before it logs its place
  (Steps.do → Printer.heading), so ""sbx"" sits above
  the start steps in the run log as on screen.
end note

note bottom of SEC
  **The heading is LEFT-anchored, unlike
  choiceui's.** There it centres on 72 columns
  over a column grid; centred here it floated at
  column 32 above text starting at 2, reading as
  a caption for nothing.
end note

note right of WIDTH
  **An unmeasurable writer gets the short form.**
  A log, a pipe or CI takes choiceui's six rules
  either side of the label rather than a rule run
  out to a guess — a hundred dashes per section
  is noise, and the width would be a pretence.
end note

note bottom of TEE
  **The section state crosses with the writer.**
  ""TeeTo"" is called after the first lines are
  out, so dropping ""pending""/""shown"" would
  redraw a heading the operator just read.
end note

note bottom of WRAP
  **The text column is fixed at two, so continuations line
  up under their own line.** A mark is one column plus a
  space in every tier; ""Notef"" and every wrapped
  continuation indent to that same column. The old
  behaviour let the terminal break lines wherever it
  liked, mid-word, at column zero.
end note

note bottom of STRIP
  **The tee strips ANSI on the way to the log, not at the
  source.** Colour is chosen once for the terminal; the
  log copy is sanitised as it is written, so the print
  sites never learn a file is listening.

  Plain is what makes that copy greppable, and only the
  SEVERITY half degrades: ""✓"" becomes ""ok: "", so a
  count of warnings in a transcript matches what a human
  saw. A role's dot is DROPPED, because a role
  classifies a layer rather than an event and there is
  nothing there worth grepping for — which is what
  Iconf's decoration did too, and the one thing about it
  that was right.
  See ""_spec/internal/runlog/run-transcript\.puml"".
end note

note bottom of DEF
  **Status goes to stderr so stdout stays machine-clean.**
  A caller piping ""proveo run --print"" gets argv and
  nothing else; the narration lands beside it on the
  other stream. ""Logf"" writes to the transcript only —
  facts worth having when troubleshooting and too noisy
  to print every run.
end note

@enduml
